**Theorem 20.2 (robust exact promised-entrance acquisition).** On every
$x\in S_B(t)$ and every legal nuisance sequence, Definition 20.1
returns exactly $x$, and hence the exact nominal block $Y=Ox$.
The actual returned field and retainable current block are

$$
v=T_t^Ix,\qquad m=Ov.
\tag{20.5}
$$

The proof includes alpha, zero, negative coordinates and unbounded
numerators. It neither acquires $B$ nor physically restores $x$.

Proof. At each actual epoch the field is $T_t^rx$. Signed floor gives
$|2^{-p}k_{r,j}-(T_t^rx)_{\ell_j}|\le2^{-p}$ for both legal bits.
Thus $\|y-Ox\|_\infty\le2^{-p}$, and $DO=\operatorname{Id}$ gives

$$
\|c-x\|_\infty\le\varepsilon<\frac1{4B^2}.
\tag{20.6}
$$

The positive $K_D$ follows from $DO=\operatorname{Id}$ on a nonempty
state space. Two distinct reduced rationals $A/d,A'/d'$ with
$d,d'\le B$ have distance
$|Ad'-A'd|/(dd')\ge1/B^2$. Each interval in (20.4) has coordinate
length $2\varepsilon<1/(2B^2)$, so contains at most one distinct
allowed rational; its actual $x_a$ is one by (20.6).
All numerator intervals are finite. In fact their lengths before
integer rounding are $2d\varepsilon<1/(2B)\le1/2$, so each $d$
contributes at most one integer. The enumeration returns the actual
coordinate finitely and uniquely. $I$ real Ticks give (20.5).
No subsequent source action is used to compute $Y$ or $m$. $\square$

This is rational separation and the existing exact forcing applied
to the actual digital service, beyond the pure-word known lattice of
§10. Different effective discrete promises can work; this particular
denominator format is sufficient and is not a universal necessary
resource.

